  "realization": [
    "periodOneOriginal inserts raw unit-period coefficients at original period-two modes2n and sets every odd mode to zero. The physical frequencies are2*pi*n andpi*k respectively.",
    "periodOneOriginalExtract reads original coefficients at mode(-(2*n)); extraction is defined on EVERY original H-minus-one potential, without physical periodicity being built into its domain.",
    "The two maps are continuous complex-linear maps with norm bounds1 and2. Their weights are equivalent, not identical. Extraction after realization is identity; realization after extraction is the entire original distribution whenever physical period one holds.",
    "The physical period-one condition, reality on every smooth test and zero physical constant mode are retained and matched to their exact raw coefficient conditions. Equality of realizations is equality of the entire smooth-test distribution.",
    "On genuine unit-period L2 inputs, realization is EXACTLY the previously verified original L2 physical operator potential with period-doubled raw coefficients. No regularity or coefficient-identification premise is left unproved."
  ],
  "spectral_continuation": [
    "Local finiteness of the actual full physical spectrum supplies arbitrarily small resolvent circles around any complex center.",
    "Every actual eigenvalue enclosed by such a circle forces the actual Riesz projection to be nonzero. Operator-norm continuity preserves nonvanishing on an original H-minus-one neighborhood, even for complex nearby potentials.",
    "If an entire closed disk lies in the original resolvent, the actual Riesz projection is zero by operator-valued Cauchy integration. No reality or spectral root-space decomposition assumption is used.",
    "High-disc trace asymptotics construct a spectral parameter with nonzero discriminant-square-minus-four at EVERY complex H-minus-one potential. Hence no identically zero characteristic slice is silently excluded by assumption.",
    "The generic zero_graph_mem_closed_of_closure_parameters proves that a jointly closed, parameter-dependent constraint on analytic zeros extends from dense parameters at a nontrivial entire slice. Local zero-count persistence includes multiple roots.",
    "Characteristic zero implies physical spectrum by applying that theorem to the actual closed joint spectral graph and dense L2 parameters.",
    "Physical spectrum implies characteristic zero: a hypothetical characteristic-free disk persists for nearby parameters; the nonzero physical projection persists too, while the L2 criterion and Cauchy theorem force the nearby projection to be zero.",
    "The resulting equivalence holds for ALL complex unit-period H-minus-one potentials, all complex energies, including zero and repeated roots. The article wrapper applies to every original real, mean-zero, physically period-one H-minus-one potential without an endpoint or finite-p membership assumption."
  ],
  "principal_declarations": [
    "NLS.KdV.singularHillSpectrum_periodOne_iff_discriminant",
    "NLS.Articles.KdVPseudomeasures.original_spectrum_iff_HminusOne_discriminant",
    "NLS.Articles.KdVPseudomeasures.originalHillDiscriminant",
    "NLS.Articles.KdVPseudomeasures.periodOneOriginal_originalPeriodOneCoefficients"
  ],
  "qualifications": [
    "This identifies spectral ZERO SETS. It does not identify actual physical algebraic multiplicities with analytic characteristic orders.",
    "No original finite-p inverse spectral membership or uniform full-isospectral bound is inferred. Theorem2.2/fullLemma2.21 and all five main theorems remain unproved.",
    "The pinned shared Miura/trace proofs are used to construct the physical H-minus-one discriminant, not to replace the original operator or to assert an NLS Birkhoff map is a KdV map."
  ],
